WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.520
On January 9th, North and South Korea engaged in high-level talks for the first time in two years.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.220
Instead of addressing North Korea's nuclear program,

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:18.680
the two sides discussed less contentious issues, such as North Korea's participation

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.400
in the upcoming Winter Olympics in Pyeongchang.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.040
While dialogue can reduce unnecessary tension, and even serve as a way to gather more information

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.920
about North Korea's perspectives and strategy, there could be drawbacks if Washington and Seoul are not careful.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.920
First, North Korea's willingness to talk about minor issues is not an indicator

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:40.020
that Pyongyang's position on nuclear weapons is softening.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.940
There is no situation under which North Korea gives up its nuclear weapons through negotiations.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:51.240
Second, Kim might be trying to improve relations with Seoul as a way of driving a wedge in the US-ROK alliance.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:56.160
And there's a risk of this, given that the United States prefers a strategy of escalating pressure

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.880
whereas South Korea prioritizes maximum engagement.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:06.100
Now more than ever the United States and South Korea must continue to coordinate their approach towards North Korea.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.700
What do you think about the potential thawing of relations between North and South Korea?

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.900
Let us know in your comments. Also, let us know what other topics

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:21.000
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

